      "text": "To determine the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we first need to calculate the area of the square and the area of one 2-by-3 rectangle.\n\nThe area of the square is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square} = 6 \\times 6 = 36\n\\]\n\nThe area of one 2-by-3 rectangle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one rectangle} = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nTo find the number of rectangles needed to cover the square, we divide the area of the square by the area of one rectangle:\n\\[\n\\text{Number of rectangles} = \\frac{\\text{Area of the square}}{\\text{Area of one rectangle}} = \\frac{36}{6} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
