      "text": "To find the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly, we need to determine the smallest side length \\( n \\) of a square such that \\( n \\) can be divided into a combination of \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangles without gaps or overlaps.\n\n### Step 1: Understanding the Problem\nWe need to cover a square region of side length \\( n \\) using \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangles. The area of the square must be equal to the total area of the rectangles.\n\n### Step 2: Area Calculation\nThe area of one \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangle is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 3 = 6 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\nLet \\( n \\) be the side length of the square. The total area of the square is:\n\\[\nn^2 \\text{ square units}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Equation for Area\nFor the square to be completely covered by \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangles, the total area of the rectangles must equal the area of the square:\n\\[\n6k = n^2\n\\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is the number of \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangles needed.\n\n### Step 4: Finding the Smallest \\( n \\)\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( n^2 \\) is divisible by 6. The prime factorization of 6 is:\n\\[\n6 = 2 \\times 3\n\\]\nThus, \\( n^2 \\) must include at least one factor of 2 and one factor of 3. We need to find the smallest \\( n \\) that satisfies this condition.\n\nThe smallest \\( n \\) that is a multiple of both 2 and 3 is 6. Let's check:\n\\[\n6^2 = 36\n\\]\n\\[\n36 = 6 \\times 6\n\\]\nThis means that 36 can be divided into 6 \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) rectangles.\n\n### Step 5: Verifying the Covering\nTo verify, we can construct the covering:\n- Place 6 rectangles side by side along one side of the square.\n- This will form a \\( 2 \\times 6 \\) rectangle, which covers the entire \\( 6 \\times 6 \\) square exactly.\n\nThus, the smallest possible number of whole \\( 2 \\)-by-\\( 3 \\) non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
