      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the smallest number of 2-by-3 rectangles that can exactly cover a square. Let's denote the side length of the square by \\( n \\).\n\nFirst, we need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( n \\) can be expressed in terms of \\( 2 \\) and \\( 3 \\) with no remainder. This means we need \\( n \\) to be a multiple of both 2 and 3, and the only such \\( n \\) is 6. We can see this because:\n\\[ 6 = 2 \\times 3 \\]\nThis means the side length of the square is 6 units. Now, we need to check if 6 can be covered by the rectangles.\n\nNext, we calculate how many 2-by-3 rectangles can fit along one side of the square. Since the side length of the square is 6, and each rectangle has a side of 2, we can fit:\n\\[ \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\]\nrectangles along one side of the square. Since the square is 6 units by 6 units, and we are using rectangles along both sides, the total number of rectangles needed is:\n\\[ 3 \\times 3 = 9 \\]\nWe can verify this by visualizing the arrangement of the rectangles. Place 3 rectangles horizontally and 3 rectangles vertically in the square, and we can see that they entirely cover the square without any gaps or overlaps.\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible number of whole 2-by-3 non-overlapping rectangles needed to cover a square region exactly is:\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]",
